WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 2.2.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.775
AMM Mathematics (x · y = k).

00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:07.907
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.866
able to by the end of this lesson

00:00:09.866 --> 00:00:12.401
you can calculate what a swap will actually

00:00:12.401 --> 00:00:15.166
pay out in a constant-product pool, and explain

00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:17.183
why bigger trades get worse prices.

00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:19.773
Here's the plan.

00:00:19.773 --> 00:00:21.458
First, the key ideas.

00:00:21.458 --> 00:00:24.173
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:24.173 --> 00:00:25.698
Then, your checklist.

00:00:25.698 --> 00:00:27.860
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:32.936
A constant-product AMM holds two tokens in a pool.

00:00:32.936 --> 00:00:34.651
It doesn't use an order book.

00:00:34.651 --> 00:00:37.705
It keeps one rule: reserves of token A ×

00:00:37.705 --> 00:00:40.386
reserves of token B = k, and k must

00:00:40.386 --> 00:00:44.261
stay constant (ignoring fees) after every trade.

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:47.386
First, Pool price is the ratio of reserves.

00:00:47.386 --> 00:00:51.678
100 ETH and 300,000 USDC means 1 ETH

00:00:51.678 --> 00:00:55.969
≈ 3,000 USDC. Next, when you add ETH

00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:00.725
to the pool, the pool must remove enough

00:01:00.725 --> 00:01:03.461
USDC to keep x·y = k.

00:01:03.461 --> 00:01:05.419
Then, the more of the pool you move,

00:01:05.419 --> 00:01:07.836
the further the price moves against you.

00:01:07.836 --> 00:01:09.711
That gap is price impact.

00:01:09.711 --> 00:01:12.858
And finally, Arbitrageurs then trade the pool

00:01:12.858 --> 00:01:15.389
back in line with other markets, and

00:01:15.389 --> 00:01:17.536
that changes what LPs hold.

00:01:17.536 --> 00:01:20.151
You'll see why that matters in Lesson 2.4.

00:01:21.451 --> 00:01:23.225
Let's look at the diagram: How

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:25.471
a constant-product swap is priced.

00:01:25.471 --> 00:01:27.031
Take a moment with it.

00:01:27.031 --> 00:01:29.441
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:30.741 --> 00:01:33.256
Let's work an example,

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:36.039
step by step. Pool: 100

00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:39.586
ETH and 300,000 U.S.D.C., so

00:01:39.586 --> 00:01:43.711
k equals 30,000,000. Spot price equals

00:01:43.711 --> 00:01:48.357
3,000 U.S.D.C./ETH. You swap in 10 ETH

00:01:48.357 --> 00:01:52.408
(fees ignored): Now try 1 ETH: x'

00:01:52.408 --> 00:01:55.862
= 101 to y' about 297,030 to

00:01:55.862 --> 00:02:01.166
you receive about 2,970 U.S.D.C., which is

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:05.541
about 1% worse. Ten times the size

00:02:05.541 --> 00:02:10.846
cost about nine times more in price impact.

00:02:10.846 --> 00:02:14.586
Size relative to pool depth is

00:02:14.586 --> 00:02:18.574
what matters, not the size of

00:02:18.574 --> 00:02:22.331
the trade in dollars. x' =

00:02:22.331 --> 00:02:26.648
100 + 10 equals 110. y' = 30,000,000

00:02:26.648 --> 00:02:32.666
divided by 110 about 272,727. You receive 300,000

00:02:32.666 --> 00:02:38.248
minus 272,727 about 27,273 U.S.D.C.. Effective price about

00:02:38.248 --> 00:02:43.358
27,273 divided by 10 equals 2,727 U.S.D.C./ETH, which

00:02:43.358 --> 00:02:46.196
is about 9.1% worse than spot.

00:02:47.495 --> 00:02:51.305
Let's look at the chart: The x·y=k curve.

00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:52.865
Take a moment with it.

00:02:52.865 --> 00:02:55.275
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:02:56.575 --> 00:02:59.507
First, check the price impact the interface shows;

00:02:59.507 --> 00:03:01.590
if it isn't shown, work it out.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.115
Next, Compare with an aggregator quote.

00:03:04.115 --> 00:03:07.745
Then, set slippage tolerance on purpose (too tight:

00:03:07.745 --> 00:03:10.328
the swap fails; too loose: you get a

00:03:10.328 --> 00:03:13.420
bad fill or a sandwich attack, see 2.5).

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.700
Also, Split large trades or use deeper pools.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.538
And finally, Run the before-signing checklist

00:03:19.538 --> 00:03:22.130
(chain, contract, token, amount, spender).

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.640
Question 1.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.561
A pool holds 50 ETH / 150,000 USDC.

00:03:28.561 --> 00:03:31.720
What is k and the spot price?

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:43.830
The answer: k = 7,500,000; spot = 3,000 USDC/ETH.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.375
Question 2.

00:03:46.375 --> 00:03:48.427
Why does a 10 ETH swap get

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:50.784
a worse average price than a 1

00:03:50.784 --> 00:03:52.760
ETH swap in the same pool?

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.415
The answer: Each unit you add moves

00:03:59.415 --> 00:04:02.022
the reserve ratio further, so later units

00:04:02.022 --> 00:04:04.495
in the same trade are priced worse.

00:04:04.495 --> 00:04:07.951
Price impact grows with trade size relative to reserves.

00:04:09.251 --> 00:04:10.521
Question 3.

00:04:10.521 --> 00:04:13.367
Who moves the pool price back in line after

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:16.213
your trade, and why does that matter to LPs?

00:04:20.633 --> 00:04:22.858
The answer: Arbitrageurs do.

00:04:22.858 --> 00:04:26.198
Their trades rebalance what the LP holds, which

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:29.678
is where impermanent loss comes from (Lesson 2.4).

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:32.224
Let's recap.

00:04:32.224 --> 00:04:34.949
Pool price is the ratio of reserves.

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:38.607
When you add ETH to the pool, the pool

00:04:38.607 --> 00:04:42.659
must remove enough USDC to keep x·y = k.

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:44.248
The more of the pool you move,

00:04:44.248 --> 00:04:46.599
the further the price moves against you.

00:04:46.599 --> 00:04:50.072
Arbitrageurs then trade the pool back in line with

00:04:50.072 --> 00:04:53.204
other markets, and that changes what LPs hold.

00:04:54.504 --> 00:04:57.019
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:57.019 --> 00:05:00.194
Next up, Lesson 2.3: Providing liquidity.
